Survey questionnaire revision means that starting in quarter 2 of 2023, it is no longer possible to separate total grocery store purchases collected in the Interview Survey into food expenditures and nonfood expenditures. In keeping with this change, the food-specific grocery expenditure Universal Classification Code (UCC) 790240 ‘food purchases at grocery stores’ ended in quarter 1 of 2023, leaving only UCC 790210 ‘all grocery purchases, including nonfood’ in quarter 2 of 2023 and beyond. Given the expanded scope of 790210, with the inclusion of nonfood grocery items, in 2023 BLS did not include 790210 ‘all grocery purchases, including nonfood’ in the food-related summary variables (FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ) on the FMLI file.\n\nThis led to the summary variables FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ being comprised only of UCC 190904, 'groceries purchased on trips.' With this change the values for FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ were much lower than previous quarters. This also had the effect on lowering expenditure amounts for the aggregate summary variables in which FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ are components:\n\nFOODPQ/FOODCQ - Total food expenditures\n\nETOTALC/ETOTALP - Total outlays\n\nETOTAPX4/ETOTACX4 - Adjusted total outlays\n\nTOTEXPPQ/TOTEXPCQ - Total expenditures\n\nTOTEX4PQ/TOTEX4CQ - Adjusted total expenditures\n\nTo try to maintain consistency from prior years, BLS has reinstated the UCC 790240 and its inclusion in the summary variables FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ. BLS has allocated 80 percent of expenses from UCC 790210, “all grocery purchases, including nonfood,” to UCC 790240, “food purchases at grocery stores.” BLS determined the 80 percent allocation using the 2022 proportion of the total expense of grocery store purchases to food purchased at grocery stores. Quarters 2 and 3 of 2023 Interview Survey microdata have been updated to reflect FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ comprising of both UCCs 190904 and 790240. Quarter 4 of 2023, as well as quarter 1 of 2024 will also reflect this update. If you downloaded data prior to September 25, 2024, please download the updated files for the corrected data. \n\nBeginning with quarter 2 of 2024, BLS will be ending the summary variables FDHOMEPQ and FDHOMECQ along with FOODPQ and FOODCQ, and will create summary variables for Groceries, GROCERYPQ and GROCERYCQ, comprised of UCCs 790210 and 190904. The new variables GROCERYPQ and GROCERYCQ along with FOODAWYPQ and FOODAWYCQ will contribute directly to the aggregate summary variables. Please note, the CE will still collect and publish food-specific grocery expenditures from the Diary Survey in the CE tables, the LABSTAT database, and the public use microdata.\n\nWe apologize for any inconvenience this has caused. If you have any questions, please don't hesitate to contact us by telephone or email.\n\nPhone – (202) 691-6900\nEmail – cexinfo@bls.gov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n____________________________ (https://code.dccouncil.gov/us/dc/council/laws/docs/18-48.pdf)\nciteturn37search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.3 years ago; § 47-2005 ... (2)  Section 47-2005 is amended by adding a new paragraph (11A) to read as ... “(i)  “Hotel” means an establishment where food and lodging are\nENROLLED ORIGINAL\nAN ACT\nCodification\nDistrict of\nColumbia\nOfficial Code\n                        \n2001 Edition\n2009 Fall\nSupp.\nIN THE COUNCIL OF THE DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA\nWest Group\nPublisher\n________________________\nTo amend Title 47 of the District of Columbia Official Code to permit an exemption from sales\ntax on utilities when directly used in a restaurant, including restaurants located in hotels.\nBE IT ENACTED BY THE COUNCIL OF THE DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA, That this\nact be cited as the “Processing Sales Tax Clarification Act of 2009”.\nAmend\n§ 47-2001\nAmend\n§ 47-2005\nSec. 2.  Title 47 of the District of Columbia Official Code is amended as follows:\n(a)  Chapter 20 is amended as follows:\n(1)  Section 47-2001(n)(1)(D) 